\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction degrades as schema complexity grows, yet prior work conflates two factors: type count and type confusability. We present what is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study isolating these factors across NER, RE, and event detection on four datasets. We quantify confusability via the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and its batch-level aggregation, the Batch Confusability Score (BCS). Degradation operates along two largely additive dimensions: count-related recall decline (up to $-$49\,pp for document-level RE) dominates confusability-related type substitution, though typical batches yield modest ${\sim}$2\,pp gaps. BCS explains 22--53\% of cross-batch RE accuracy variance under RE's forced-choice format but shows no batch-level signal for NER, where errors scatter across missed entities and boundary failures. Few-shot demonstrations widen the confusability gap (+4.3\,pp under 5-shot). Schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification (NER) and routing failure (RE). Pairwise confusability is a schema-structural property: effects replicate across model scales and families, and persist under varied prompt designs.
\end{abstract}

