\section*{Limitations}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Model coverage.} Primary experiments use Qwen3-32B with cross-model validation on Llama-3.1-8B, 70B-Instruct (FP8), and Gemini 2.5 Flash (API). Dimension~1 has been validated across all four models including a proprietary one; Dimension~2 has been verified only on open-weight models and was not robustly detectable on NER at 8B scale, likely due to floor effects.

\item \textbf{Effect size and statistical design.} Pairwise SCS explains 1.3--1.7\% of type confusion variance; entity frequency, prompt position, and mention ambiguity likely account for the remainder. The count $\times$ BCS interaction is bounded under the linear specification (TOST $p = 0.003$ at $\pm 3$\,pp on FewRel; DocRED inconclusive, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}) but inconclusive on the logit scale ($p = 0.209$). BCS-controlled experiments use a single seed with greedy decoding; the residual SD of 0.88\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:variance-decomposition}) supports robustness but precise magnitudes may vary.

\item \textbf{Task format dependency.} BCS batch-level prediction is demonstrated only under RE's forced-choice classification format. An unconstrained decoding ablation ($n = 25$) confirms this: removing the enum constraint eliminates the BCS--accuracy correlation ($\rho = -0.14$, $p = 0.49$), establishing that the batch-level signal is specific to forced-choice RE. NER exact-match evaluation conflates boundary and type errors, and relaxed matching may yield different confusability effect sizes.

\item \textbf{Causal mechanism.} The study establishes correlational evidence with controlled stratification; mechanistic analysis at the attention or logit level is needed to confirm the causal pathway. A post-hoc renaming experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:renaming}) confirms this concern: replacing high-SCS labels with semantically distinct paraphrases reduced pairwise embedding similarity but decreased accuracy by 3--9\,pp, as label unfamiliarity introduced errors that outweighed any confusability reduction.

\item \textbf{Data contamination.} FewRel (2018) may appear in pretraining corpora, but train-origin relations show numerically lower accuracy than val-origin ($-8.8$\,pp, n.s.), opposite to the contamination hypothesis. DocRED (2019) provides a lower-contamination-risk replication with consistent slopes ($R^2 = 0.53$; Appendix~\ref{app:split-origin}).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} Cross-dataset comparisons serve as independent replications and do not require family-wise correction \citep{rothman-1990}. Within-dataset secondary analyses involve multiple looks; we report individual $p$-values and note that core claims rest on $p < 0.001$ \citep{dror-2018-significance,card-2020-power}.

