\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Training-free LLM IE.}
Training-free LLM IE spans instruction tuning \cite{instructuie-2023}, code-style schema encoding \cite{knowcoder-2024,codeie-2023}, annotation-guided extraction \cite{gollie-2024,adelie-2024}, schema-driven decoding \cite{slot-2025,oneke-2025}, and constrained JSON decoding \cite{json-schema-bench-2025,willard2023efficient}. For NER, methods include zero-shot decomposition \cite{xie-zeroshot-ner-2023,promptner-2023}, distillation \cite{uniner-2024}, bidirectional encoders \cite{gliner-2024,gliner2-2025}, and fine-grained entity typing \cite{gillick-2014-entity-typing}. For RE, recent work examines zero-shot relation triplet extraction via prompting \cite{relationprompt-2022} and revisits relation extraction in the LLM era \cite{wadhwa-re-llm-2023}. These methods perform well with moderate schemas (4--20 types) but none investigates how performance degrades as schema complexity scales, nor proposes quantitative metrics for inter-type confusability. \citet{wei-2023-chatie} reformulate zero-shot IE as multi-turn QA with ChatGPT but do not analyze schema-size effects.

\paragraph{Schema complexity in IE.}
The most directly related work is LLM-PEE \cite{llm-pee-2025}, which partitions large event schemas by type embedding similarity. Our work differs in three ways: (1)~we conduct controlled analysis to understand \emph{why} extraction degrades rather than developing an engineering solution; (2)~our BCS-controlled experiments isolate count from confusability, which LLM-PEE conflates; (3)~our SDGD experiments reveal that merge amplification (not partitioning quality) is the bottleneck in decompose-then-merge pipelines. LLM-PEE's union merge suffers the same precision collapse (0.56$\to$0.21 in NER) that our \S\ref{sec:sdgd} analysis identifies as the fundamental bottleneck. Other approaches address schema complexity via hard negative mining \cite{iepile-2024}, schema definition optimization \cite{parse-2025}, multi-agent debate \cite{crossagentie-2025,cmas-ner-2025}, schema-aware decomposition for event extraction \cite{dee-2024}, and active prompting \cite{reflect-learn-2026}; all treat it as an engineering problem rather than isolating the underlying degradation factors. Concurrently, \citet{chen-2026-multi-instance} show that instance count degrades LLM multi-instance processing more than context length, consistent with our count-dominant finding. Concurrently, \citet{haystack-to-needle-2025} reduces label spaces via iterative LLM filtering, \citet{dlisc-2025} caches schemas for on-device extraction, and \citet{spt-2025} treats schemas as parameterized tools. Our framework explains \emph{why} such reduction works: it implicitly lowers batch confusability.

\paragraph{Schema complexity in dialogue and IE.} The Schema-Guided Dialogue dataset~\cite{rastogi2020towards} and SGD-X~\cite{lee2022sgd} document performance degradation with schema linguistic variation. \citet{li2023evaluating} observe count-dependent degradation in ChatGPT IE. Prompt order sensitivity \cite{lu2022fantastically} and label ordering effects are well documented; our prompt-variant experiments (\S\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_variants}) control for such artifacts. \citet{volkanovska2025ner} categorize LLM NER errors including type confusion. The relationship between label similarity and classification confusion has precedent in traditional ML: hierarchical classification suffers sibling confusion proportional to inter-class proximity \citep{deng-2010-imagenet}, label confusion matrices improve text classification \citep{guo-2021-label-confusion}, classification confusion captures semantic similarity \citep{zhou-2025-rethinking}, label smoothing exploits inter-class similarity structure \citep{muller2019when}, and human label uncertainty mirrors model confusion patterns \citep{peterson2019human}. Hierarchical label sets \citep{novack-2023-chils}, enriched type descriptions \citep{cocchieri-2025-zeroner}, and universal entity taxonomies \citep{yang2025beyond} address related problems but are orthogonal to confusability-aware batching.

\paragraph{SCS novelty.} SCS shares its formal definition (pairwise cosine similarity of type name embeddings) with the partitioning criterion in LLM-PEE~\cite{llm-pee-2025}. The use of embedding distance for type discrimination has parallels in metric learning \citep{snell2017prototypical} and zero-shot classification \citep{yin-2019-zero-shot-bench}. Our contribution is the controlled experimental framework that isolates confusability's predictive relationship with extraction degradation. The modest pairwise $r^2$ (1.3--1.7\%) is consistent with individual-difference predictors in behavioral research \citep{cohen-1988-power,lakens-2013-effect-size} and compounds at the aggregate level. Our centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}) connects to representation degeneration \cite{gao-repr-degen-2019,ethayarajh-contextual-2019}: local clusters can be isotropic \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}, explaining why MAVEN's single-ontology types conflate confusability with centrality.

