\section{Schema Decomposition: Failure, Diagnosis, and Mitigation}
\label{sec:sdgd}

A natural intervention against confusability-related degradation is to partition the schema so confusable types never co-occur. We test this with \textbf{Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition} (SDGD), which applies greedy graph coloring on the SCS graph to assign types to low-confusability batches.

\paragraph{NER: merge amplification.}
All decomposition modes fall below the single-pass NER baseline (F1\,=\,0.445): SDGD obtains 0.251, random batching 0.320, per-type 0.292 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}). The core failure is \emph{merge amplification}: presenting text to the model $k$ times with different type subsets is associated with precision collapse (0.563$\to$0.210--0.249) as false positives scale with $k$, while true positives remain bounded. Confidence gating does not recover net F1. RE avoids this failure because closed-set classification requires no span reconciliation across passes.

\paragraph{RE: oracle routing.}
Oracle routing achieves 0.802 accuracy versus 0.504 single-pass on FewRel 80-relation RE (+29.8\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:batch_composition}); type separation recovers performance absent merge amplification. Confusability-aware grouping yields only +1.4\,pp over random ($p \approx 0.19$, n.s.): graph coloring achieves only marginal mean BCS reduction (0.559$\to$0.552), insufficient to produce detectable accuracy improvement. All practical routing strategies fall below the single-pass baseline (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_routing}).

Promising avenues include coarse-to-fine pipelines and retrieval-based routing.

